  "settingsCardGroups": "Deck settings",
  "settingsCardGroupsHint": "A deck is a tag: give a tag a daily new/review cap and its whole subtree counts as that deck; grading mode and card type are changed here too.",
  "settingsQuestionBankHint": "Question overview, intake, paper assembly, photo cleanup and material intake all start here.",

  "settingsUsageDoc": "Usage guide",
  "settingsUsageDocHint": "How each part works and what its limits are: AI, review, knowledge base, question bank, photos/materials, cognitive model, ranking",
  "docAiTitle": "What the assistant can do, and what waits for your consent",
  "docAiBody": "The AI can only change data through tools, and every tool carries a risk level: read-only calls (query_tasks, query_schedule, query_content, fetch_page, evaluate_problem_fit, compose_paper, find_similar_questions, find_questions_by_capability, plan_today, review_metrics and friends) run immediately; writes (creating tags, flashcards, tasks, documents, material intake, applying today's plan...) wait for your consent in both the per-action and the plan-first modes; deletions ask every single time and can be undone. You see what a call will change before it runs and get one undo after it; the model never sees your API key, and skill scripts do not receive it either.",
  "docReviewTitle": "Review and flashcards",
  "docReviewBody": "Flashcards hang off tags, and the tag tree is the deck: a daily new/review cap set on one tag covers its whole subtree (no row at all means no cap). Due dates come from FSRS alone - the cognitive model only changes what today's queue puts first. Grading can be strict, lenient or self-assessed; lenient ignores typos and word order, and a card you got wrong ignores the daily cap and comes back the same day. Deck settings live in Settings -> Deck settings (also reachable from the review screen's app bar). Cards with no tag belong to no group, so no daily target can reach them: the deck settings screen shows how many such cards exist and offers a one-tap \"put them all in one group\" fix, and the new-flashcard dialog can take a group up front.",
  "docKnowledgeTitle": "Knowledge base and tags",
  "docKnowledgeBody": "Tags form a tree, with / separating the levels. Deleting a tag also deletes its whole subtree together with the flashcards and review history inside it (the AI's delete tool asks for each one and leaves an undoable snapshot). Both card shapes - cards generated from a template and cards created directly per unit - count on the cognitive readings page, and the orphan banner there cleans up only when you press its button.",
  "docBankTitle": "Question bank and paper assembly",
  "docBankBody": "The intake page turns pasted text or picked images into drafts for proofreading: the AI only structures them, and nothing is written until you press save, while an identical question (same stem fingerprint) points at the existing row instead of duplicating it. The paper page assembles a paper only from questions you already have (at-risk and developing points first); when it cannot fill the paper it lists the missing knowledge points honestly - the AI never writes questions. The paper can be copied out as printable plain text. On the cognitive model page the capability-move profile opens a read-only list of questions whose chains used a move.",
  "docMaterialTitle": "Photos, materials and rehearsal",
  "docMaterialBody": "Photo intake turns one photo into four kinds of proposals - extra tags, flashcards, MindNet knowledge-point nodes and questions transcribed verbatim - and writes nothing before you confirm; material intake turns a piece of material into knowledge points / cards / connections, again only writing after you confirm, with an undo trail. A photo can also become a run/1 request carrying mechanism parameters: the mechanism panel lets you try or rehearse it, and a rehearsal only reports readings without touching your data. Saying \"do not install the goal mechanism\" really leaves it out.",
  "docTodayTitle": "What to do today, and when",
  "docTodayBody": "Today's list shows the cards due today; the rhythm layer reports alpha(tau) attention availability; conflicting tasks and schedule blocks are flagged and can be pushed back with one tap; the cognitive ranking tier only changes which card comes first, never due dates, and is recomputed only when you press the sort button. Counterfactual V(pi) and the retention probe likewise only affect ordering.",
  "docCognitiveTitle": "How to read the cognitive model",
  "docCognitiveBody": "Cognitive model in settings is a read-only readings page: R is current memory strength, R0 is the strength right after learning, Sigma is accumulated effort, and the developing-zone / dead-end column is the tierB diffusion verdict on the tag graph (starts are the most recently reviewed points, targets are the ones due today); with no tags or no usable start and target it reads unavailable, which is not the same as healthy. \"Danger\" comes from the metacognition rows: it appears only when your self-rated confidence and your actual recall differ enough, and a missing verdict is not the same as healthy. The capability-move profile groups stuck risk by the moves on solved chains and answers which kind of move to practise; tapping a row lists the questions whose chains used it (read-only). At the top of the readings page there is a read-only knowledge-network view that draws knowledge points and tags as one graph (colour = cognitive zone, size = card count); tap a node to read it. The statements at the top record that it does not move due dates, that its parameters are uncalibrated and that it does not decide for you. The AI's evaluate_problem_fit tool reads the same numbers to judge whether a question is worth doing now.",
  "docFormulaTitle": "How the score is computed",
  "docFormulaBody": "Total = urgency x weight + goal match x weight + state fit x weight + expected pressure x weight - fatigue penalty x weight. Every component is between 0 and 1, the weights are normalized internally, so the total is between 0 and 1 as well. The order is only recomputed when you press the sort button - nothing changes in the background.",
  "docCurrentWeights": "Current weights (shipped values in brackets)",
  "docWeightsNormalized": "Edited weights are normalized automatically: even if they do not add up to 1, the total never exceeds 1 because the ranker rescales by their sum.",
  "docStatusTitle": "Event bar edge colours",
  "docStatusBody": "Green = completed; blue = waiting (expected moment not reached); yellow = expected moment has passed; red = deadline has passed. The colour only expresses state and never changes the ordering.",

